  {
    "id": "CVE-2024-9121",
    "issues": [
      "363538434"
    ],
    "impacts": [
      "Inappropriate implementation in V8 in Google Chrome prior to 129.0.6668.70 allowed a remote attacker to potentially perform out of bounds memory access via a crafted HTML page. Reporter-observed symptom: `Fatal error in ../../src/parsing/parser.cc, line 1120 # Debug check failed: class_info.static_elements_function_id == initializer_id (3 vs. 2)`. Root cause: `ParserBase::EnsureStaticElementsScope` allocated the synthesized class-static initializer's `function_literal_id` lazily, *after* the parser had already descended into the computed property name's expression. For a class like `class C { static [function(){}] = ...; }`, the inner `function(){}` literal consumed an id first, so the static-elements initializer scope ended up with an id one greater than the SFI expected. On lazy reparse via `Parser::ParseClassForMemberInitialization`, the seeded counter expected the static-elements id to be the *first* id consumed during the reparse. The DCHECK at `parser.cc:1260` (mainline) / `parser.cc:1120` (12.9 branch) — `class_info.static_elements_function_id == initializer_id` — fires with the advisory's `3 vs. 2` signature."
    ],
    "itw": false,
    "commits": [
      "https://chromium.googlesource.com/v8/v8/+/8068f489ec2c7e9de15e179c8c25b45224f7f96f"
    ],
    "references": [
      "https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/v8/v8/+/5850479"
    ],
    "releases": [
      {
        "name": "Chrome 129.0.6668.70 - 2024-09-24",
        "date": "2024-09-24",
        "advisory": "https://chromereleases.googleblog.com/2024/09/stable-channel-update-for-desktop_24.html"
      }
    ]
